          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What TSH range does guidance describe as the treatment threshold in subclinical hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "For subclinical hypothyroidism, there is broad agreement to treat when thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is persistently above approximately 10 mIU/L, according to the Endocrinology and Thyroid Association guidance.\n\nHowever, guidance differs for TSH levels between the upper reference limit and 10 mIU/L. Some guidance suggests a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present. Other guidance recommends monitoring alone for most people in this range, citing trials in older adults that showed no symptomatic benefit from treatment.\n\nAdditionally, authorities disagree on whether to adjust the TSH reference range upward for older adults, as a mildly raised value may be considered normal for age. Pregnancy is treated with separate, lower, trimester-specific ranges.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
